{
  "title": "ABP Framework \u2014 SaaS (14,266\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: ABP Framework\nDomain: SaaS | Scale: large | Stars: 14,266\nProblem: ABP bridges the gap between ASP.NET Core and real-world enterprise application requirements by providing an opinionated architecture with best practices, infrastructure, and pre-built modules to automate repetitive work and enable developers to focus on business logic.\nTarget users: Enterprise software developers, ASP.NET Core teams, SaaS platform builders, Microservices architects\nCore features: Domain-Driven Design architecture support; Modular monolith and microservices patterns; Multi-tenancy support; Pre-built application modules (Account, Identity, CMS Kit); Event bus and background job system; Audit logging, authorization, validation, and exception handling; BLOB storing and data filtering infrastructure; UI themes and startup templates for multiple frameworks\nIntegrations: ASP.NET Core, Entity Framework Core, Microsoft Identity, Angular, Blazor, NuGet package ecosystem\nTech stack: Angular, Blazor, TypeScript + ASP.NET Core, C# + Multiple (EF Core support) + Event bus, background jobs, BLOB storage, audit logging\nKey libraries: Volo.Abp.Core, Volo.Abp.Account, Volo.Abp.Identity, Volo.Abp.CmsKit, Entity Framework Core\nArchitecture: modular-monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Opinionated framework design with first-class support for DDD, modularity, and multi-tenancy reduces boilerplate and enforces best practices across enterprise applications\n- Pre-built modules and startup templates accelerate development by providing production-ready infrastructure for common concerns like authentication, authorization, and audit logging\n- Multi-pattern support (monolith, microservices, modular) with comprehensive tooling and documentation enables teams to scale from simple to complex architectures without framework migration",
